s.\n\nHer grandmother Rose's voice came back to her: 'The heart knows first, baby. The head just takes minutes.' Emma Hartley stopped running. She turned around and went to find him."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Choice', 'text': "a handwritten letter got a place of honor on the mantel — not as a relic, but as a reminder: that love lost can be love found, that home is a person, and that home was never a town at all.\n\nher overbearing mother left town with the gift wrap and the almosts. Emma Hartley felt nothing but pity — and then not even that, because her arms were full of present tense.\n\nShe found him at the shore, where it started. 'I choose,' she said, breathless, 'the thing that scares me. I choose you. I've always chosen you — I just took seventeen years to say it.'"}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise', 'text': 'home is a person — Emma Hartley wrote it in the new journal, the one for the next seventeen years. In Willow Creek, under the stars, with love: the whole story, and only just beginning.\n\nThey danced on the porch to the mixtape — no, to a new playlist, because the past is a place to visit, not to live. Emma Hartley had learned that at last.\n\nHe proposed with a handwritten letter — fitting, her neighbor Mrs. Abernathy said, since it started everything. Emma Hartley said yes before he finished asking, because seventeen years is long enough to wait for a sentence.'}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue in Wildflowers', 'text': "home is a person. In Willow Creek, in the wildflowers, in the salt air, in every sunset: the whole story, still beginning, never ending.\n\nher neighbor Mrs. Abernathy still diagnoses everything with pie. Her grandmother Rose's quilt is worn thin with love. her overbearing mother is a story they tell laughing, a ghost with no haunting left in it.\n\nEmma Hartley walks the shore most evenings, sometimes alone, sometimes with him, always with Willow Creek around her like a held breath. The sunsets haven't changed. She has — into someone who